$DIR/main.p:5:2: hits is written here with no lock that guards it, while another task may use it
    hits is written at main.p:5 in handle by the task started by task handle at
    main.p:12 with no lock held, and another task started there writes it at
    main.p:5 in handle. Two tasks may run these at the same time, one of them
    writing, which is a data race: the other may see a value half written, or
    lose an update.
    The compiler infers which lock guards shared data from the locks held where
    it is written: a lock that every write holds. Here no one lock is held at
    every write.
    Go leaves data races to a run-time detector that a program must be run
    under, and a racing Go program can see values half written. Plutonium tasks
    run in parallel with no such detector, so safe code must be free of data
    races, and the compiler checks it from the program itself, with no
    annotations.
    Hold one sync.Mutex at every access, writes and reads (a sync.RWMutex's
    RLock is enough for reads):
        mu.Lock()
        hits = v
        mu.Unlock()
    Or, for a number, use an atomic type from pluto/sync/atomic, such as
    atomic.Int64, and its methods at every access.
    (see "Data race freedom" in the Plutonium spec, section 19.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3502" for a longer explanation)
